1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,943
Book Three, Proposition Twenty-Four.

2
00:00:02,943 --> 00:00:07,919
Similar segments of circles on equal segments are equal to one another.

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:00:14,086
Let A E B and C F D be similar segments of circles standing on equal chords A B and C D.

4
00:00:14,086 --> 00:00:18,010
We are to prove that segment A E B equals segment C F D.

5
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:26,949
Apply the segment A E B to C F D by placing point A on point C and the straight segment A B along C D.

6
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:31,960
Because A B equals C D, point B also coincides with point D.

7
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:44,372
With base A B coinciding with base C D, if segment A E B does not coincide with C F D, it must either fall wholly within it, fall wholly outside it, or fall awry across it as C G D.

8
00:00:44,372 --> 00:00:54,641
It cannot fall wholly inside or outside, for by Three Twenty-Three two similar and unequal circle segments cannot stand on the same chord on the same side.

9
00:00:54,641 --> 00:01:04,977
Nor can it fall awry as C G D, for then two circles would intersect at more than two points—C, D, and their crossing point—which is impossible by Three Ten.

10
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:17,300
Therefore segment A E B coincides everywhere with segment C F D, and by Common Notion Four, coinciding figures are equal; so segment A E B equals segment C F D, as was required to prove.

11
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:26,194
Just as Book One, Proposition Eight follows from Proposition Seven by superposition, Proposition Twenty-Four follows immediately from Twenty-Three.

12
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:40,316
Heath highlights Euclid's Greek verb parallassein—to fall awry—which separates two distinct impossibilities: nested arcs are ruled out by Three Twenty-Three, while crossing arcs as C G D are ruled out by Three Ten's two-point intersection limit.

13
00:01:40,316 --> 00:01:51,960
